    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-007",
      "SourceSection": "21.2: A Rational River",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Attribution: Family boating, by publicdomainpictures. Public Domain. publicdomainpictures. Source.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-008",
      "SourceSection": "21.2: A Rational River",
      "SourceExcerpt": "If the speed of the river is \\(r\\), write an expression for the time it takes to travel 5 kilometers upstream and an expression for the time it takes to travel 10 kilometers downstream.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-009",
      "SourceSection": "21.2: A Rational River",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Use your expressions to calculate the speed of the river. Explain or show your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-010",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Circuits in parallel follow this law: The inverse of the total resistance is the sum of the inverses of each individual resistance. We can write this as: \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{R_T}=\\frac{1}{R_1} +\\frac{1}{R_2}+ . . . + \\frac{1}{R_n}\\)…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-011",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Two circuits are placed in parallel. The first circuit has a resistance of 40 ohms and the second circuit has a resistance of 60 ohms. What is the total resistance of the two circuits?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Two circuits are placed in parallel. The second circuit has a resistance of 150 ohms more than the first. Write an equation for this situation showing the relationships between \\(R_T\\) and the resistance \\(R\\) of the first circuit.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For this circuit, Clare wants to use graphs to estimate the resistance of the first circuit \\(R\\) if \\(R_T\\) is 85 ohms. Describe how she could use a graph to determine the value of \\(R\\) and then follow your instructions to find \\(R\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Two circuits with resistances of 40 ohms and 60 ohms have a combined resistance of 24 ohms when connected in parallel. If we had used two circuits that each had a resistance of 48 ohms, they would have had that same combined resistance. 48…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Harmonic mean of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\dfrac{2ab}{a+b}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Arithmetic mean of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The harmonic mean of 40 and 60 was 48, and their arithmetic mean is (40+60)/2=50. Experiment with other pairs of numbers. What can you conclude about the relationship between the harmonic mean and arithmetic mean?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A boat travels about 6 kilometers per hour in still water. If the boat is on a river that flows at a constant speed of \\(r\\) kilometers per hour, it can travel at a speed of \\(6+r\\) kilometers per hour downstream and \\(6-r\\) kilometers per…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "On one particular river, the boat can travel 4 kilometers upstream in the same amount of time it takes to travel 12 kilometers downstream. Since time is equal to distance divided by speed, we can express the travel time as either…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align} \\frac{12}{6+r} &= \\frac{4}{6-r} \\\\ \\frac{12}{6+r} \\boldcdot (6+r)(6-r) &= \\frac{4}{6-r} \\boldcdot (6+r)(6-r) \\\\ 12(6-r) &= 4(6+r) \\\\ 72-12r &= 24 + 4r \\\\ 48 &= 16r \\\\ 3 &= r \\\\ \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Substituting this value into the original expressions, we have \\(\\frac{12}{6+3}=\\frac43\\) and \\(\\frac{4}{6-3}=\\frac43\\), so these two expressions are equal when \\(r=3\\). This means that when the water flow in the river is about 3…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L21-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "21.3: Rational Resistance",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Even though we started out with a rational expression on each side of the equation, multiplying each side by the product of the denominators, \\((6+r)(6-r)\\), resulted in an equation similar to ones we have solved before. Multiplying to get…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l21.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
